        "## Monte Carlo Portfolio Simulation\n",
        "\n",
        "This notebook demonstrates a basic Monte Carlo simulation to project future portfolio values. The simulation uses historical daily returns (or simulated returns) to create thousands of possible future price paths for a given portfolio, considering its asset allocation and the historical drift and volatility."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "34ce3a25"
      },
      "source": [
        "# Import necessary libraries\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "import seaborn as sns"
      ],
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "78d29318"
      },
      "source": [
        "### 1. Define Portfolio and Simulation Parameters\n",
        "\n",
        "For this example, we'll create a hypothetical portfolio with a few assets. In a real scenario, you would load historical data for your chosen assets and calculate their daily returns, mean returns, and standard deviations."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "990d35ab",
        "outputId": "be5be333-32e4-4298-d7b4-b83ae2342c9c"
      },
      "source": [
        "# --- Portfolio Definition (Example Data) ---\n",
        "# In a real scenario, you would fetch actual historical data for your assets.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Example asset names\n",
        "assets = ['Asset_A', 'Asset_B', 'Asset_C']\n",
        "\n",
        "# Example portfolio weights (must sum to 1)\n",
        "weights = np.array([0.5, 0.3, 0.2])\n",
        "\n",
        "# Example historical daily returns data (simulated for demonstration)\n",
        "# In reality, this would come from a DataFrame of actual historical returns.\n",
        "np.random.seed(42) # for reproducibility\n",
        "num_days_historical = 252 * 5 # 5 years of daily data\n",
        "\n",
        "historical_returns = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    'Asset_A': np.random.normal(0.0005, 0.01, num_days_historical),\n",
        "    'Asset_B': np.random.normal(0.0007, 0.015, num_days_historical),\n",
        "    'Asset_C': np.random.normal(0.0003, 0.008, num_days_historical)\n",
        "})\n",
        "\n",
        "# Calculate daily mean returns (drift) and daily standard deviation (volatility)\n",
        "mean_daily_returns = historical_returns.mean()\n",
        "std_daily_returns = historical_returns.std()\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Mean Daily Returns:\")\n",
        "print(mean_daily_returns)\n",
        "print(\"\\nStandard Deviation of Daily Returns:\")\n",
        "print(std_daily_returns)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Initial portfolio value\n",
        "initial_portfolio_value = 100000\n",
        "\n",
        "# --- Monte Carlo Simulation Parameters ---\n",
        "num_simulations = 10000 # Number of possible future scenarios to simulate\n",
        "num_trading_days = 252 # Number of trading days in one year (e.g., for a 1-year forecast)"
      ],
      "execution_count": null,
